.TH PLANG 1 "@PLANG_MANPAGE_DATE@" "plang @PLANG_VERSION_STRING@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plang \- LLVM-based Pascal compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plang
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plang
is a Pascal compiler built on the LLVM compiler infrastructure.
It accepts a Pascal source file and compiles it to a native executable,
object file, assembly, or LLVM IR depending on the options given.
.P
The compiler follows the clang/flang driver model: the
.B plang
binary acts as a driver that orchestrates the Pascal front end
.RB ( plang\ \-pc1 ),
the LLVM code generator
.RB ( llc ),
and the linker.
.P
Which linker depends on the target.  On ELF targets it is
.BR ld.lld ,
which must be on
.BR PATH ,
and the link uses the startup files of the host GCC installation.  On macOS it
is the system
.BR ld ,
found through
.BR xcrun (1),
and the link uses the macOS SDK; nothing need be installed beyond the Xcode
command line tools.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Output modes"
.TP
.B \-S
Emit native assembly instead of an executable.
The default output file is \fI<source>.s\fR.
.TP
.B \-c
Compile to an object file; do not link.
The default output file is \fI<source>.o\fR.
.TP
.B \-emit\-llvm
Emit LLVM IR text instead of running the back end.
The default output file is \fI<source>.ll\fR.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the primary output to \fIfile\fR instead of the default name.
.SS "Optimization"
.TP
.BR \-O0 " / " \-O1 " / " \-O2 " / " \-O3
Set the optimization level passed to
.BR llc .
The default is
.BR \-O0 .
.SS "Run-time checks"
.TP
.BR \-frange\-checks " / " \-fno\-range\-checks
Emit, or omit, the checks that an array index lies within its index type
(ISO 7185 §6.5.3.2) and that a value assigned to a subrange lies within it
(§6.6.3.2, §6.8.2.2).  On by default.  A program compiled with
.B \-fno\-range\-checks
is not one whose errors are reported, and the flag is a statement by whoever
compiles it that the program is known not to commit them.
.TP
.BR \-fnil\-checks " / " \-fno\-nil\-checks
Emit, or omit, the check that a pointer being dereferenced is not
.BR nil
(§6.5.4).  On by default.  Without it the dereference reaches the hardware and
the program is stopped by a signal, with nothing said about where.  This is
separate from
.BR \-fno\-range\-checks ,
which took it too until 0.1.2: a bounds test inside a loop and a single compare
against null are not the same cost, and asking to be rid of the one says
nothing about the other.
.PP
Division by zero and a
.B case
statement matching no label are always checked; neither costs anything
measurable.
.SS "Diagnostic language"
.TP
.BI \-fdiagnostics\-language= tag
Print diagnostics in the language named by
.IR tag ,
a POSIX locale name such as
.BR fr ,
.B fr_CA
or
.BR es_MX .
.B C
and
.B en
select the built\-in English.  A region falls back to its language, so
.B fr_CA
uses
.I fr_CA.po
if there is one and
.I fr.po
otherwise; a language plang has no catalog for falls back to English, as does a
catalog that is missing, unreadable or incomplete.
.IP
Without this option,
.BR LC_ALL ,
.B LC_MESSAGES
and
.B LANG
are consulted in that order.  This option overrides all of them.
.IP
Only the messages are affected.  plang never calls
.BR setlocale (3):
its scanner decides what a letter and a digit are, so a locale that changed
those would change which programs it accepts, and what a compiler compiles must
not depend on the environment it is run in.
.TP
.B \-fdiagnostics\-show\-fuzzy
Use translations marked
.B "#, fuzzy"
in the catalog, which are otherwise ignored.  A fuzzy entry is one no reviewer
has approved, and an unreviewed guess at what a compiler error means is worse
than the English; this exists so that whoever reviews a translation can read it.
.SS "Language"
.TP
.BI \-std= dialect
Select the Pascal language dialect.
.RS
.TP
.B iso7185
ISO 7185 Standard Pascal at level 1 (default, fully implemented).  Level 1 is
level 0 together with conformant array parameters, in both the
.B array
and the
.B "packed array"
form, so a procedure may take an array of any length:
.RS
.PP
.EX
procedure show(s: packed array [lo..hi: integer] of char);
.EE
.RE
.IP
The extensions Extended Pascal adds \(em
.BR string( n ),
.BR ** ,
.BR substr ,
.BR card ,
.BR halt ,
.BR >< ,
.BR "for ... in" ,
an
.B otherwise
part of a case-statement, and the rest \(em are rejected under this dialect.
.TP
.B iso10206
ISO 10206 Extended Pascal (implemented).
.TP
.B turbo
Turbo Pascal extensions (in development; not all features are implemented yet).
.TP
.B delphi
Delphi / Object Pascal (planned; not yet implemented).
.TP
.B fpc
Free Pascal extensions (planned; not yet implemented).
.RE
.PP
.B \-std
is enforced strictly: a construct outside the selected standard is an error and
not a warning, so there is no separate switch for insisting on it.
.SS "Warnings"
Every warning is enabled by default.  A warning reports something the standard
permits a processor to pass over — an error clause 5.1 f) 1) allows to go
unreported, or a construct that is well formed and cannot have been meant — so
none of them rejects a program unless
.B \-Werror
is given.
.TP
.BI \-ferror\-limit= n
Report at most
.I n
errors and pass over the rest, which spares the cascade a single mistake early
in a file can set off.  The compilation still fails.  Zero, the default, means
no limit.  Warnings are not counted against it.
.TP
.B \-w
Suppress all warnings.
.TP
.B \-Werror
Treat all warnings as errors.
.TP
.B \-Wall
Enable every warning.  They are on already; the flag is accepted so that a
command line written for another compiler need not be edited.
.TP
.BI \-W name
Enable the named warning, undoing an earlier
.BI \-Wno- name \fR.
.TP
.BI \-Wno- name
Disable the named warning.
.TP
.B \-fcolor\-diagnostics
Colorize diagnostics even when standard error is not a terminal, which is what
a build log read back through a pager wants.
.TP
.B \-fno\-color\-diagnostics
Do not colorize diagnostics.  Neither option is needed in the ordinary case:
color is used when standard error is a terminal and not otherwise.  The driver
and the front end are separate processes and both print diagnostics, and the
option settles it for both.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-warnings
List the warning names.
.PP
The warnings, by the name each answers to:
.TP
.B var\-uninitialized
A variable is read on a path that has not given it a value (§6.5.1).
.TP
.B for\-var\-after\-loop
The control variable of a for-statement is read after the statement has
finished, which §6.8.3.9 leaves undefined.
.TP
.B result\-not\-always\-set
A function has a path through it that does not assign its result (§6.6.2).
That it is assigned somewhere is a separate requirement, and an error.
.TP
.B const\-div\-zero
The divisor of
.BR / ,
.B div
or
.B mod
is a constant zero, which §6.7.2.2 makes an error wherever it is reached.
.TP
.B const\-out\-of\-range
A constant assigned to a variable of a subrange type lies outside it, which the
run-time check is certain to catch wherever it is reached.
.TP
.B unreachable\-code
No path reaches a statement.
.TP
.B compare\-always
One operand's type settles a comparison on its own, so the other is not being
consulted.  Only a range the program wrote down — a subrange or an enumeration
— counts, since every char lies within
.BR chr(0)..chr(255) .
.TP
.B case\-not\-exhaustive
A case-statement has no
.B otherwise
part and does not cover its selector's type, and §6.8.3.5 makes reaching it
with an unmatched value an error.  Checked only for a selector whose type
states its range: a subrange, an enumeration, or boolean.
.TP
.B unused\-variable
A variable is declared and never mentioned again.
.TP
.B unused\-parameter
A parameter is never named in the body.  A procedure passed as a procedural
parameter (§6.6.3.1) takes the signature its formal demands whether it wants
every parameter or not, so some correct programs will want this one off.
.TP
.B label\-unreachable
A label is defined but no
.B goto
names it.
.TP
.B unrecognized\-argument
An argument beginning with
.B \-
matches no option.  It is passed over rather than rejected, since a build
system handing plang a flag meant for another compiler is a common enough
thing, but
.B \-Werror
makes it fatal.  This is the one warning about the command line rather than
about the program, and the only one that can be reported before a source file
has been opened.
.PP
The three that depend on following the flow through a block —
.BR var\-uninitialized ,
.B for\-var\-after\-loop
and
.B result\-not\-always\-set
— report only where no path reaching the statement gives the variable a value.
They say nothing at all about a block that declares a label, since a
.B goto
may land anywhere the label is in scope, nor about a variable a nested
procedure can reach, nor about a program that has already failed to compile.
Reading a variable that has no value stays an error this processor does not
report in general; see
.BR docs/conformance.md .
.SS "Debugging"
.TP
.B \-g
Generate source-level debug information.
.SS "Linker control"
.TP
.BI \-\-target= triple
Generate code for the specified target triple (e.g.\&
.I aarch64-linux-gnu
or
.IR arm64-apple-macosx15.0 ).
The host triple is used when this option is omitted.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Add \fIdir\fR to the linker library search path.
.TP
.BI \-l lib
Link with the library \fIlib\fR.
.TP
.BI \-Wl, args
Pass the comma-separated list \fIargs\fR directly to the linker.
.TP
.BI \-Xlinker " arg"
Pass \fIarg\fR as a single argument to the linker.
.SS "Diagnostic / informational"
.PP
A diagnostic names the file, the line and the column, then quotes the line and
puts a caret under the column:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
prog.pas:4:13: error: undefined identifier 'coutn'
    writeln(coutn)
            ^
.fi
.RE
.PP
The line is reproduced as it was written, tabs included, so the caret lands
under the right character whatever the indentation.
.PP
A diagnostic with no place in the source is printed under the program name
instead of a file, a line and a column.  These are the ones about the command
line or about the toolchain, and they are the driver's:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
plang: error: no input files
.fi
.RE
.PP
They pass through the same machinery as the rest, so
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-Werror ,
.BI \-Wno- name
and
.BI \-ferror\-limit= n
all apply to them.
.TP
.B \-v
Show the sub-commands that
.B plang
would run and use verbose output from each tool.
.TP
.B \-###
Print the sub-commands that would be run without actually executing them.
.TP
.B \-save\-temps
Keep intermediate
.I .ll
and
.I .o
files rather than deleting them after compilation.
.TP
.B \-dumpversion
Print the compiler version number and exit.
.TP
.B \-dumpmachine
Print the compiler's default target triple and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h " / " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the compiler version, target, and thread model, then exit.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
Consulted in that order for the diagnostic message language, unless
.B \-fdiagnostics\-language=
is given.  A value of
.B C
or
.B POSIX
means the built\-in English.  Nothing else about plang is locale\-sensitive.
.TP
.B PLANG_LOCALE_DIR
Directory to search for message catalogs before the one installed beside the
compiler.  For trying out a translation without installing it.
The first two apply when linking for an ELF target, the rest when linking for
macOS.
.TP
.B PLANG_GCC_DIR
Override the GCC installation directory used to locate CRT objects for linking.
.TP
.B PLANG_LIB_DIR
Override the system C library directory used to locate
.I Scrt1.o
and related CRT files.
.TP
.B SDKROOT
Override the macOS SDK to link against.  When it is not set the SDK is the one
.BR xcrun (1)
reports.
.TP
.B MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET
The earliest macOS the executable is to run on, for example
.IR 15.0 .
This is recorded in both the object file and the executable.  When it is not
set the deployment target is taken from the target triple.
.TP
.B PLANG_BUILTINS_LIB
Override the path to the compiler builtins archive
.RI ( libclang_rt.osx.a ).
It supplies the helper routines the compiler emits calls to rather than code
for, which for Pascal means the complex multiply and divide; on ELF targets
these come from
.IR libgcc .
When it is not set the archive is looked for beside the linker.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile and link a Pascal program:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plang hello.pas \-o hello
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compile to an object file only:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plang \-c hello.pas \-o hello.o
.EE
.RE
.PP
Emit LLVM IR for inspection:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plang \-emit\-llvm hello.pas \-o hello.ll
.EE
.RE
.PP
Optimize and compile:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plang \-O2 program.pas \-o program
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what commands would be run without running them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
plang \-### program.pas \-o program
.EE
.RE
.SH CONFORMANCE
plang complies with the requirements of level 1 of ISO/IEC 7185.
.PP
Clause 5.1 of that standard asks a processor to be accompanied by documents
defining its implementation-defined features and describing its extensions.
.I docs/conformance.md
in the distribution is those documents: it defines
.BR maxint ,
the character set and its ordering, the accuracy of real arithmetic, the
default field widths and the representation a real is written in, and the
binding of a file program-parameter; it says what happens at each point the
standard leaves implementation-dependent, such as the order operands are
evaluated in; and it lists which of the standard's
.I errors
are reported and which are not.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR llc (1),
.BR ld.lld (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR xcrun (1)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at \fIhttps://github.com/apprenticewiz/plang/issues\fR.
.SH AUTHORS
The plang developers.
